<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kido Takayoshi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Katsura Kogorō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kido Takayoshi (1833–1877), also known as Katsura Kogorō, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Chōshū domain and one of the three great leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. He negotiated the Satchō Alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> in 1866 and helped draft the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-oath">Charter Oath</a> of 1868. In the new government he pressed for the return of the domains to the emperor and their abolition in 1871, and he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwakura-mission">Iwakura Mission</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kido-takayoshi.jpg" alt="Kido Takayoshi" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takayoshi_Kido_suit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kido was born Wada Kogorō on 11 August 1833 in Hagi, in the Chōshū domain, the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> physician. At the age of seven he was adopted into the Katsura family and became known as Katsura Kogorō. His adoptive father was on his deathbed, and the family's stipend was cut from 150 koku to 90.</p><p>He was educated at the Meirinkan, and in 1849 at the Shōka Sonjuku, the academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shōin</a>, from whom he took the philosophy of imperial loyalism. In 1852 he went to Edo to study swordsmanship and artillery, made ties with radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> from the Mito Domain, and later supervised the building of the domain's first western-style warship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After 1858 Katsura served at the domain's Edo residence as a liaison between the bureaucracy and the radical young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonno-joi">Sonnō jōi</a> movement. He was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> forces drove Chōshū out in the coup of 30 September 1863. His diary says that he was at the loyalist meeting at the Ikedaya inn on 8 July 1864 but left before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinsengumi">Shinsengumi</a> attacked; rumours told other stories.</p><p>He was involved in the Hamaguri Gate rebellion of 20 August 1864, though not present; some 28,000 houses burned and his adopted son was among the Chōshū dead. Using the new name Kido, he was instrumental in the Satchō Alliance of 1866 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Ōkubo Toshimichi</a>, mediated by Sakamoto Ryōma.</p><p>After 1868 Kido helped draft the Five Charter Oath and directed the abolition of the han system. He opposed hereditary privilege and favoured appointment by merit. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iwakura-mission">Iwakura Mission</a>, which left on 23 December 1871, and on returning on 13 September 1873 he advocated constitutional government. He resigned in 1874 in protest at the Taiwan Expedition, returned after the Osaka Conference of 1875, and became chairman of the Assembly of Prefectural Governors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kido died on 26 May 1877, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a>, of cancer; his last words to Ōkubo were reported as &quot;Enough Saigō.&quot; With Saigō and Ōkubo he was known as one of the Ishin-no-Sanketsu, the Three Great Nobles of the Restoration. His diary shows his conflict between loyalty to Chōshū and the interest of the nation, and it has been published in English translation. He was enshrined at Kyoto Ryozen Gokoku Shrine and at the Kido Shrine in Yamaguchi, and his house in Hagi, a National Historic Site since 1934, is a memorial museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kido Takayoshi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1865 Takasugi Shinsaku and the Kiheitai militia ousted Chōshū’s conservatives, and on 7 March 1866 Sakamoto Ryōma brought <b>Kido Takayoshi</b> and Saigō Takamori together in the Satsuma–Chōshū Alliance.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Its men, <b>Kido Takayoshi</b>, Ito Hirobumi and Yamagata Aritomo, led Meiji Japan; it became Yamaguchi Prefecture in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hagi, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.41,131.4&amp;year=1861" aria-label="Hagi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7891 917 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kido Takayoshi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8341 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8341 1187h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.41,131.4&amp;year=1861">What was here in 1861? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
